::ALG1_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which number is less: -8 or -3? {
=-8 #Correct. On a number line, -8 is farther left than -3, so -8 is less.
~-3 #This confuses larger absolute value with greater value. For negative numbers, the number farther left is less.
~3 #This ignores the negative signs. The question compares -8 and -3, not positive 3.
~8 #This changes the sign and does not answer the comparison between the two given numbers.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -6 + 14. {
~20 #This adds the absolute values and keeps the result positive without considering opposite signs.
~-20 #This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign, but the positive value has the larger absolute value.
=8 #Correct. The signs are different, so subtract 14 - 6 = 8. The positive number has the larger absolute value.
~-8 #This subtracts correctly but uses the wrong sign. Since 14 is farther from zero than -6, the result is positive.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 5 - (-9). {
~-4 #This treats subtraction of a negative as ordinary subtraction. Subtracting a negative means adding the opposite.
=14 #Correct. 5 - (-9) = 5 + 9 = 14.
~4 #This subtracts 5 from 9 and misses the meaning of subtracting a negative.
~-14 #This uses the correct distance but the wrong sign. Adding 9 to 5 gives a positive result.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate (-4)(-7). {
~-28 #This multiplies correctly but uses the wrong sign. Same signs make a positive product.
=28 #Correct. A negative times a negative is positive, and 4 × 7 = 28.
~-11 #This adds the numbers instead of multiplying them.
~11 #This uses addition reasoning instead of multiplication reasoning.
}

::ALG1_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L02_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> + <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>. {
~-<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> #This subtracts correctly but keeps the wrong sign. The positive fraction has the larger value.
=<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> #Correct. -<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> = -<sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>, and -<sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> + <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>.
~<sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> #This adds the absolute values instead of combining opposite-signed rational numbers.
~-<sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> #This adds the absolute values and keeps a negative sign, but the signs are different.
}
